==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|præsently}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|present|ly|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|British}} {{IPA|/ˈpɹɛzəntli/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-presently.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

#{{lb|en|now|_|British|rare}} [[immediately|Immediately]], at once; [[quickly]]. {{defdate|from 14<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#*'''~1600''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Hamlet}}'', Act 3, {{nowrap|Scene 2:}}
#*:''Polonius'': My lord, the queen would speak with you, and '''presently'''.
#*''{{w|The Bible}}'': {{w|Gospel of Matthew|Matthew}} xxi. 19:
#*:And '''presently''' the fig tree withered away.
#*{{RQ:Flr Mntgn Essays}}, Folio Society, 2006, Vol.1, p.219:
#*:the butler supposing the Wine had beene so carefully commended unto him for the goodnesse of it, imediately presented some unto the Pope, who whilest he was drinking, his sonne came in and never imagining his bottles had beene toucht, tooke the cup and pledged his father, so that the Pope died '''presently'''; and the sonne, after he had long time beene tormented with sicknesse, recovered to another worse fortune.
#Before long; [[soon]]. {{defdate|from 15<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#*'''1646''', {{w|Thomas Browne}}, ''{{w|Pseudodoxia Epidemica}}'', I.3:
#*:It{{...}}is the greatest example of lenity in our Saviour, when he desired of God forgiveness unto those, who having one day brought him into the City in triumph, did '''presently''' after, act all dishonour upon him, and nothing could be heard but, ''Crucifige'', in their Courts.
#*{{quote-book|year=1907|author=[[w:Robert W. Chambers|Robert Chambers]]
|chapter=1/2|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL8127246W The Younger Set]
|passage=[…] '''presently''' Selwyn lay prone upon the nursery floor, impersonating a ladrone while pleasant shivers chased themselves over Drina, whom he was stalking.}}
#*'''1940''', {{w|Raymond Chandler}}, ''{{w|Farewell, My Lovely}}'', Penguin 2010, p.55:
#*:‘I shall '''presently''' be getting a call to tell me of that.’
#At the [[present]] time; [[now]]; [[currently]]. {{defdate|from 15<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#*Sir {{w|Philip Sidney}} (1554-1586)
#*:The towns and forts you '''presently''' have.
#*'''1891''', [http://books.google.com/books?id=8OYGAAAAYAAJ&source=gbs_navlinks_s The Welsh Review, No.1 (November 1891)]. "A Word to the Welsh People." p.1:
#*:To all of you, therefore, who call Wales your motherland, whether you '''presently''' inhabit some other portion of the globe or breathe the air of your cloud-kissed country{{nb...}}.
#{{ll|en|obsolete}} With actual [[presence]]; in [[actuality]]. {{defdate|~1600}}
#*Bishop {{w|Stephen Gardiner}} (1483-1555)
#*:His precious body and blood '''presently''' three.

====Usage notes====
* Some older usage guides, especially for UK English, object to the sense {{term|now}}, though most major modern dictionaries do not.

